"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:10,252\nOnline, blended, face to face\n\n4\n00:00:13,052 --> 00:00:14,574\nOnline, face to face,\n\n5\n00:00:14,594 --> 00:00:16,251\nor a blend of both.\n\n6\n00:00:16,595 --> 00:00:18,906\nEvery institution has had to choose.\n\n7\n00:00:19,163 --> 00:00:21,449\nWhich one helps students learn the most?\n\n8\n00:00:21,619 --> 00:00:25,718\nA 2010 report for the US Department of Education,\n\n9\n00:00:26,052 --> 00:00:27,702\nled by Barbara Means,\n\n10\n00:00:27,991 --> 00:00:31,451\nsearched more than a thousand studies of online learning.\n\n11\n00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,354\nIt kept only rigorous comparisons that measured learning,\n\n12\n00:00:35,731 --> 00:00:38,826\nyielding fifty contrasts for meta-analysis.\n\n13\n00:00:38,996 --> 00:00:45,296\nFully online versus fully face to face: an effect size of about zero point zero five.\n\n14\n00:00:45,781 --> 00:00:47,547\nEssentially no difference.\n\n15\n00:00:47,917 --> 00:00:52,350\nBlended versus face to face: about zero point three five,\n\n16\n00:00:52,503 --> 00:00:55,431\na meaningful advantage for blended courses.\n\n17\n00:00:55,601 --> 00:00:58,339\nBut the authors warned against crediting the medium.\n\n18\n00:00:58,894 --> 00:01:02,649\nThe blended conditions often included extra learning time,\n\n19\n00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:06,097\nextra resources, and more chances to practice.\n\n20\n00:01:06,533 --> 00:01:11,334\nIn their words, the advantage reflected redesigning the learning experience,\n\n21\n00:01:11,565 --> 00:01:13,475\nnot the technology itself.\n\n22\n00:01:13,645 --> 00:01:14,578\nSome caution.\n\n23\n00:01:15,121 --> 00:01:19,416\nThe studies ran through 2008 and were mostly with older learners,\n\n24\n00:01:19,589 --> 00:01:21,408\nwith very few in schools.\n\n25\n00:01:21,772 --> 00:01:24,321\nAnd they studied planned online courses,\n\n26\n00:01:24,549 --> 00:01:28,077\nnot the emergency remote teaching of 2020,\n\n27\n00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:29,644\nwhich was a different thing.\n\n28\n00:01:29,814 --> 00:01:31,868\nThe lesson echoes Series five.\n\n29\n00:01:32,381 --> 00:01:34,485\nFormat matters less than structure.\n\n30\n00:01:34,895 --> 00:01:38,948\nA well designed online course beats a poorly designed lecture,\n\n31\n00:01:39,165 --> 00:01:41,949\nand a well designed blend can beat both.\n\n32\n00:01:42,119 --> 00:01:42,883\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:43,259 --> 00:01:44,980\nWhen you move material online,\n\n34\n00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,293\nredesign it rather than recording it.\n\n35\n00:01:47,705 --> 00:01:52,254\nUse online time for what it does well,\n\n36\n00:01:52,794 --> 00:01:55,099\nlike self paced practice with feedback.\n\n37\n00:01:55,243 --> 00:01:57,202\nUse in person time for discussion,\n\n38\n00:01:57,608 --> 00:01:59,487\nproblem solving and questions.\n\n39\n00:01:59,736 --> 00:02:02,515\nOne tip to share with students: treat online work as real class time.\n\n40\n00:02:02,685 --> 00:02:04,409\nIt's not where the course happens.\n\n41\n00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:05,838\nIt's how it's built.\n\n"